\" content=\"35179487\" />\n  \n    <meta name=\"ncbi_uid\" content=\"35179487\" />\n  \n\n\n\n  <!-- Social meta tags for unfurling urls -->\n  \n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"35179487\"><meta name=\"robots\" content=\"index,nofollow,noarchive\"><meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"35179487\"><meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35179487/?format=pubmed\"><meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"35179487\"><meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/persistent/pubmed-meta-image-v2.jpg\"><meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/persistent/pubmed-meta-image-v2.jpg\"><meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\"><meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"PubMed\"><meta name=\"twitter:domain\" content=\"pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"><meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\"><meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"35179487\"><meta name=\"twitter:url\" content=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35179487/?format=pubmed\"><meta name=\"twitter:description\" content=\"35179487\"><meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/persistent/pubmed-meta-image-v2.jpg\">\n\n\n  <!-- OpenSearch XML -->\n  <link rel=\"search\" type=\"application/opensearchdescription+xml\" href=\"https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/persistent/opensearch.xml\" title=\"PubMed search\">\n\n  <!-- Disables severely broken elements when no JS -->\n  <noscript>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" href=\"https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/a2ffdabe-e220-486a-83d1-09afbe4369a3/core/no-script.css\">\n  </noscript>\n\n  \n    <link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35179487/?format=pubmed\" />\n  \n\n\n</head>\n<body >\n\n  \n  <div class=\"article-page\" id=\"article-page\">\n    \n      <pre class=\"article-details\" id=\"article-details\">PMID- 35179487\r\nOWN - NLM\r\nSTAT- MEDLINE\r\nDCOM- 20220429\r\nLR  - 20240824\r\nIS  - 2050-084X (Electronic)\r\nIS  - 2050-084X (Linking)\r\nVI  - 11\r\nDP  - 2022 Feb 18\r\nTI  - Hypertrophic chondrocytes serve as a reservoir for marrow-associated skeletal \r\n      stem and progenitor cells, osteoblasts, and adipocytes during skeletal \r\n      development.\r\nLID - 10.7554/eLife.76932 [doi]\r\nLID - e76932\r\nAB  - Hypertrophic chondrocytes give rise to osteoblasts during skeletal development; \r\n      however, the process by which these non-mitotic cells make this transition is not \r\n      well understood. Prior studies have also suggested that skeletal stem and \r\n      progenitor cells (SSPCs) localize to the surrounding periosteum and serve as a \r\n      major source of marrow-associated SSPCs, osteoblasts, osteocytes, and adipocytes \r\n      during skeletal development. To further understand the cell transition process by \r\n      which hypertrophic chondrocytes contribute to osteoblasts or other marrow \r\n      associated cells, we utilized inducible and constitutive hypertrophic chondrocyte \r\n      lineage tracing and reporter mouse models (Col10a1CreERT2; Rosa26(fs-tdTomato) \r\n      and Col10a1Cre; Rosa26(fs-tdTomato)) in combination with a PDGFRa(H2B-GFP) \r\n      transgenic line, single-cell RNA-sequencing, bulk RNA-sequencing, \r\n      immunofluorescence staining, and cell transplantation assays. Our data \r\n      demonstrate that hypertrophic chondrocytes undergo a process of dedifferentiation \r\n      to generate marrow-associated SSPCs that serve as a primary source of osteoblasts \r\n      during skeletal development. These hypertrophic chondrocyte-derived SSPCs commit \r\n      to a CXCL12-abundant reticular (CAR) cell phenotype during skeletal development \r\n      and demonstrate unique abilities to recruit vasculature and promote bone marrow \r\n      establishment, while also contributing to the adipogenic lineage.\r\nCI  - © 2022, Long et al.\r\nFAU - Long, Jason T\r\nAU  - Long JT\r\nAUID- ORCID: 0000-0002-6006-0932\r\nAD  - Department of Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United \r\n      States.\r\nAD  - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, \r\n      United States.\r\nFAU - Leinroth, Abigail\r\nAU  - Leinroth A\r\nAD  - Department of Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United \r\n      States.\r\nAD  - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, \r\n      United States.\r\nFAU - Liao, Yihan\r\nAU  - Liao Y\r\nAD  - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, \r\n      United States.\r\nAD  - Department of Pharmacology and Cancer Biology, Duke University School of \r\n      Medicine, Durham, United States.\r\nFAU - Ren, Yinshi\r\nAU  - Ren Y\r\nAD  - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, \r\n      United States.\r\nFAU - Mirando, Anthony J\r\nAU  - Mirando AJ\r\nAD  - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, \r\n      United States.\r\nFAU - Nguyen, Tuyet\r\nAU  - Nguyen T\r\nAUID- ORCID: 0000-0002-8769-9955\r\nAD  - Program of Developmental and Stem Cell Biology, Duke University School of \r\n      Medicine, Durham, United States.\r\nFAU - Guo, Wendi\r\nAU  - Guo W\r\nAD  - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, \r\n      United States.\r\nAD  - Department of Pharmacology and Cancer Biology, Duke University School of \r\n      Medicine, Durham, United States.\r\nFAU - Sharma, Deepika\r\nAU  - Sharma D\r\nAD  - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, \r\n      United States.\r\nFAU - Rouse, Douglas\r\nAU  - Rouse D\r\nAD  - Division of Laboratory Animal Resources, Duke University School of Medicine, \r\n      Durham, United States.\r\nFAU - Wu, Colleen\r\nAU  - Wu C\r\nAD  - Department of Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United \r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"curl -s 'https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/search?q=%22hypertrophic%20cartilage%20zone%22&ontology=uberon'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"response\":{\"docs\":[{\"iri\":\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0008187\",\"ontology_name\":\"uberon\",\"ontology_prefix\":\"UBERON\",\"short_form\":\"UBERON_0008187\",\"description\":[\"see also VSAO:0000098\"],\"label\":\"hypertrophic cartilage zone\",\"obo_id\":\"UBERON:0008187\",\"type\":\"class\"},{\"iri\":\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0006772\",\"ontology_name\":\"uberon\",\"related_synonyms\":[\"hypertrophic chondrocyte zone\",\"long bone hypertrophic chondrocyte zone\",\"maturation zone\",\"zone of hypertrophic chondrocytes\"],\"ontology_prefix\":\"UBERON\",\"short_form\":\"UBERON_0006772\",\"description\":[\"The layer of the epiphyseal plate of a long bone where chondrocytes mature and enlarge[MP].\"],\"label\":\"long bone epiphyseal plate hypertrophic zone\",\"obo_id\":\"UBERON:0006772\",\"type\":\"class\",\"exact_synonyms\":[\"epiphyseal plate hypertrophic zone\"]},{\"iri\":\"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000743\",\"ontology_name\":\"uberon\",\"ontology_prefix\":\"UBERON\",\"short_form\":\"CL_0000743\",\"description\":[\"A chondrocyte that is part of the hypertrophic cartilage zone. This cell is significantly enlarged and characterised by high expression of type X collagen (COL10A1) in both humans and mice. It actively coordinates endochondral ossification by mineralising the extracellular matrix, attracting blood vessels via angiogenic signalling, and mediating the transition from cartilage to bone - often by transdifferentiating into an osteoblast rather than undergoing apoptosis.\",\"Despite historical descriptions as \\u0027terminally differentiated,\\u0027 hypertrophic chondrocytes survive and transdifferentiate into osteoblasts. Genetic lineage tracing shows that hypertrophic chondrocytes do not simply undergo apoptosis but cross the cartilage–bone boundary, dedifferentiate into skeletal stem/progenitor cells, and then redifferentiate into osteoblasts and osteocytes. Single-cell RNA-seq further indicates that these chondrocyte-derived SSPCs are a major source of trabecular osteoblasts and marrow adipocytes.\"],\"label\":\"hypertrophic chondrocyte\",\"obo_id\":\"CL:0000743\",\"type\":\"class\"}],\"numFound\":3,\"start\":0},\"responseHeader\":{\"QTime\":119,\"status\":0},\"facet_counts\":{\"facet_fields\":{\"ontologyPreferredPrefix\":[\"UBERON\",\"3\",\"ADDICTO\",\"0\",\"ADO\",\"0\",\"AEO\",\"0\",\"AFO\",\"0\",\"AGRO\",\"0\",\"AISM\",\"0\",\"AMPHX\",\"0\",\"APO\",\"0\",\"APOLLO_SV\",\"0\",\"ARO\",\"0\",\"AfPO\",\"0\",\"BAO\",\"0\",\"BCIO\",\"0\",\"BCO\",\"0\",\"BFO\",\"0\",\"BMONT\",\"0\",\"BSPO\",\"0\",\"BTO\",\"0\",\"CAO\",\"0\",\"CARO\",\"0\",\"CCF\",\"0\",\"CCO\",\"0\",\"CDAO\",\"0\",\"CDNO\",\"0\",\"CHEBI\",\"0\",\"CHEMINF\",\"0\",\"CHIRO\",\"0\",\"CHMO\",\"0\",\"CIDO\",\"0\",\"CIO\",\"0\",\"CL\",\"0\",\"CLAO\",\"0\",\"CLO\",\"0\",\"CLYH\",\"0\",\"CMO\",\"0\",\"CMPO\",\"0\",\"COB\",\"0\",\"COLAO\",\"0\",\"COVOC\",\"0\",\"CPONT\",\"0\",\"CRO\",\"0\",\"CTENO\",\"0\",\"CVDO\",\"0\",\"DDANAT\",\"0\",\"DDPHENO\",\"0\",\"DHBA\",\"0\",\"DIDEO\",\"0\",\"DISDRIV\",\"0\",\"DMBA\",\"0\",\"DOID\",\"0\",\"DRON\",\"0\",\"DUO\",\"0\",\"EC\",\"0\",\"ECAO\",\"0\",\"ECO\",\"0\",\"ECOCORE\",\"0\",\"ECTO\",\"0\",\"EDAM\",\"0\",\"EFO\",\"0\",\"EMAPA\",\"0\",\"EMI\",\"0\",\"ENM\",\"0\",\"ENSGLOSS\",\"0\",\"ENVO\",\"0\",\"EPIO\",\"0\",\"EUPATH\",\"0\",\"EXMO\",\"0\",\"ExO\",\"0\",\"FAO\",\"0\",\"FBbi\",\"0\",\"FBbt\",\"0\",\"FBcv\",\"0\",\"FBdv\",\"0\",\"FIDEO\",\"0\",\"FLOPO\",\"0\",\"FMA\",\"0\",\"FOBI\",\"0\",\"FOODON\",\"0\",\"FOVT\",\"0\",\"FYPO\",\"0\",\"GALLONT\",\"0\",\"GAZ\",\"0\",\"GECKO\",\"0\",\"GENEPIO\",\"0\",\"GENO\",\"0\",\"GEO\",\"0\",\"GNO\",\"0\",\"GO\",\"0\",\"GSSO\",\"0\",\"GeXO\",\"0\",\"HANCESTRO\",\"0\",\"HAO\",\"0\",\"HBA\",\"0\",\"HCAO\",\"0\",\"HOM\",\"